# Lecture 11E

## Chromosome Abnormalities: Duplications

## Chapter 9

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 17, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Explain how unequal crossing over creates duplications.
- Relate pigment-gene changes to color vision.
- Describe how duplication contributes to gene evolution.
- Connect copy-number changes with gene dosage.

=== layout: section

# Chromosome Alterations

=== layout: one-panel

# *Alterations of Chromosome Structure*

@body

- Breakage of a chromosome can lead to four types of changes in chromosome structure
  - [*Deletion*]{underline=true} removes a chromosomal segment
  - [*Duplication*]{color=red underline=true} [repeats a segment]{color=red}
  - [*Inversion*]{underline=true} reverses orientation of a segment within a chromosome
  - [*Translocation*]{underline=true} moves a segment from one chromosome to another

=== layout: section

# Duplications

=== layout: one-panel

# *Duplication*

@body

![Slide image 1](assets/lect11e-chromo_alter_2-duplications/duplication_overview.drawn.svg)

A duplication repeats a chromosomal segment.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Unequal Crossing Over*

@body

![Slide image 2](assets/lect11e-chromo_alter_2-duplications/unequal_tandem_crossover.drawn.svg)

- A [*tandem duplication*]{underline=true} is one in which the duplicated segment is in the same orientation immediately adjacent to the normal region in the chromosome.
- Tandem duplications can produce more duplicated copies by [*unequal crossing over*]{underline=true}.

